### Adapting the Output

MiDaS returns disparity values up to an unknown constant ($d_0$), which must be transformed into world coordinates ($X,Y,Z$) for each pixel. To translate the returned disparity $d[n,m]$ into depth values $z[n,m]$, we invert the disparity after adding the constant:

$$\begin{aligned}z [n,m ] = \frac{1}{ d [n,m ] + d_0}
\end{aligned}$$

Assuming a pinhole camera and using the depth $z[n,m]$ and the camera matrix $\mathbf{K}$, we obtain the 3D coordinates in the camera reference frame as:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y \\ 
Z 
\end{bmatrix}
= z[n,m ] \times \mathbf{K}^{-1} \times
\begin{bmatrix}
n \\
m \\ 
1 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The camera matrix $\mathbf{K}$ accounts for focal length, image size, camera center, and rotation: $\mathbf{K} = \mathbf{M} \mathbf{R}$. The intrinsic parameters $\mathbf{M}$ are defined by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{M}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
a & 0 & n_0\\
0 & a & m_0 \\ 
0 & 0 & 1 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}$$

[FIGURE:2c6cb957a032955d]

The extrinsic parameters $\mathbf{R}$ model the 3D rotation, primarily around the $X$-axis:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & \cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\ 
0 & \sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}$$

To apply this, we use heuristic settings: $d_0 = 0$, $n_0 = N/2$, $m_0 = M/2$, and $a = N$ (where $N$ is image width).

